    "result_text": "I added `require_setting(key)` to `hc/front/decorators.py`. It returns a 404 `HttpResponse` unless `settings.<key>` is truthy, and otherwise calls the view normally. I haven't run the Django tests or exercised a view with the setting on and off.\n\nThe file already had the decorator's imports but no function. Integration views such as Slack, GitHub, Apprise and VictorOps already import `require_setting` from `hc.front.decorators`, so I didn't touch any of them. You use it like this:\n\n```python\n@require_setting(\"SLACK_ENABLED\")\ndef slack_help(request): ...\n```\n\nIt's the catalog's `views.require_setting@v1` pattern, with the `# pcat:` marker kept above the function. `check_code` on the full file reports no deviations from the pattern. My first check, on a snippet without imports, showed a high-severity \"library swapped\" hit. That came from the missing imports and cleared once I checked the whole file.\n\nThe key must exist in Django settings. A missing one raises `AttributeError` (a 500) instead of a 404, because `getattr` has no default. That is how the pattern is written, so I left it."
  },
